**Lemma 6.2 (rigidity of the enlarged continuing task).** For $h,k\in\Omega$,
equality of the at-most-one-primitive $Q_+$ response maps implies $h=k$.
Conversely equal histories give equal complete finite-experiment maps.

Proof. Every cut in this domain has a successful lawful next action.
At a non-IN use its prescribed Advance, including the ordinary old Ready
continuation rather than optional Install. At a variable IN choose a next
bit from a witnessing canonical completion. At a code IN choose its fixed
canonical next bit. The dispatcher is continuing: after finite source or
service return another canonical frame may be appended. RCA Theorem 15.3
and AIF Lemma 2.7 provide these lawful successors throughout installation
and busy phases. Missing hypothetical external supply is not an executed
refusal, and progress here asserts existence of a lawful supplied action,
not unconditional eventual completion.

Choose such an action at $h$. Equal partial maps require that action at
$k$ to be defined and successful with equal complete successors; `none`
or undefined cannot equal it. By CSA.3 and Lemma 3.4 the structural
successors decode to the same $Y$. Lemmas 5.3–5.4 identify their whole
successor histories as equal. Removing the last occurrence gives $h=k$.
This avoids treating a fixed-code IN as an extra variable-frame bit or
silently treating Advance as Install. Equal histories conversely have
identical raw states, actual permissions, names and future partial domains;
determinism gives equal labels, outputs, prices and successors along every
allowed finite/adaptive experiment. This is SSA's one-step rigidity
argument on the instantiated larger image. It uses complete successors
and does not assert rigidity for public outputs alone. ∎

